  "discussions_data": [
    {
      "id": "c55322d6e6c89df9636353ab038831d740bf65d1",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 445321711,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> We know that \"000eversion 1\\n\" is valid since it is from the spec.\n> But would something with two spaces before the digit also be valid, such as\n> \"000fversion  1\\n\" ? If the latter, then we would need to have much more complex string parsing.\n> Or what about \"0010version 001\\n\" ? is that valid?\n>\n> This is probably a question to ask Jonathan. Unless I have missed something, which is likely since I have only browsed through the specs and not read it carefully, I can't find something that would make these other examples invalid.\n\nFrom the spec:\n\n```\n  advertised-refs  =  *1(\"version 1\")\n                      (no-refs / list-of-refs)\n                      *shallow\n                      flush-pkt\n```\n\nThat's a literal string, so it indeed requires exactly one space character.",
          "author": {
            "id": 7512212,
            "username": "jrnieder",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Jonathan Nieder",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/e44c1b17e16c337952b533877e3463349679a20280b2e9796a9929539f0d3a6e?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jrnieder"
          },
